WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.490
Mickey Finnegan, Filmmaker: But I also did
pick up a tip that I applied to myself and

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:08.380
it really worked for me as I was watching
an interview with Taika Waititi I actually

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:10.030
and he was talking about his writing process.

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:15.110
Film Courage: How often have you been afraid
to finish a screenplay?

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:24.190
Mickey: All the time. I feel like with every
screenplay there's always stages you go through.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:28.150
At least for me, this is my experience going
through a screenplay and usually it's like

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:33.750
you have an idea and you're very excited about
it and you start to put it all together and

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.900
you're thinking Yeah this could actually be
good. You've convinced yourself that's going

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:42.050
to be good and then you start to write it
and when you start to write it's really exciting

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:46.460
because you're like Oh it's finally happening
and I’m setting things up and I’ve got

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:51.640
this character in here and I'm getting it
getting it going and then you know when you

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.850
get to the end of the screenplay you're also
very excited because you're like oh I'm so

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:58.890
close to the finish line oh like this is this
is great but you know in the middle when you're

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:04.650
in that middle slog of getting through a screenplay
that's the point when you're start to think

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:09.850
like like I don't know is this idea actually
that good like I'm not sure if this is working

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:14.930
the way that I intended it to work and that's
when you can start to have those fears of

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:21.070
like maybe not wanting to finish something
or feeling like you know I think maybe this

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:28.240
isn't gonna be worth the rest of my time to
finish so for me personally I think everything

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.840
I write I usually go through that like you
know experience or usually go through that

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.980
that doubt but but I always try to remind
myself to like you know trust the process

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:43.880
a little bit and think you know there's a
reason why you were excited about this there's

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:49.310
a reason why you just start you decided to
start this at one point you know a couple

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:54.630
months ago you felt like this was an amazing
idea so just you know trust the process you

00:01:54.630 --> 00:02:01.090
know just keep going get to the end and and
you know hopefully when you do finish it and

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:07.590
you read it back you'll you'll get excited
about it again so but yeah back to your question

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:11.850
I think all the time I'm always like fearful
of finishing a script because I'm always like

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:16.530
oh is this thing going to be bad with the
wrestling script that you had option with

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:21.210
your partner was there a moment where were
there times when the two of you didn't feel

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:27.120
like was it really worth it yeah there were
there were times where I was like oh I don't

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.700
really know if it was working or or not but
maybe that one was a little bit different

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:38.620
because that one we were together so you have
someone to kind of constantly talk talk it

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:44.640
through but other scripts that i've written
are those ones are the ones where it's like

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.390
yeah you only have that internal conversation
with yourself so those are the ones that are

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:53.750
sometimes that can be a little harder to push
through are there unfinished screenplays at

00:02:53.750 --> 00:03:00.190
your house there there's two types of unfinished
screenplays there's the unfinished screenplays

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:07.210
that I started way early in my career when
I just started writing and and didn't really

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:12.150
know what I was doing so there's those and
then there's the ones that I'm currently working

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:19.020
on that are unfinished but they will be finished
so I think now I'm at the point where if I

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:26.530
really do start to write something I definitely
will finish it so i've kind of tricked myself

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:32.989
into into getting into the routine of of making
sure that I get across that finish line what

00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:37.680
do you find time to write since you're doing
these music videos is it between each project

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:43.709
I know we've had some time off with the current
pandemic but yeah yeah I think writing is

00:03:43.709 --> 00:03:48.750
just something that i've just kind of built
into my schedule it's something I just try

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:53.069
to do a few times a week I mean if I'm if
I'm busy if I'm directing something or I'm

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:59.459
on a project like you know the week gets away
from myself and yeah I don't I don't I don't

00:03:59.459 --> 00:04:05.319
get mad if I can't write that day it's okay
but typically you know if I have a day off

00:04:05.319 --> 00:04:11.060
I'm definitely in the morning writing for
a few hours every day so it's just something

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:16.150
I kind of just build into my routine what
does the inner dialogue sound like when doubt

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:22.030
creeps in about finishing a screenplay oh
it sounds like oh this is you know all the

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:30.210
things this is terrible this this this is
not working you know this idea is really stupid

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:35.180
you know you can say all those things as you're
writing and working on something and feeling

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:39.919
like well like why am I spending my time writing
this thing like no one's gonna read this or

00:04:39.919 --> 00:04:45.449
you know it's it won't be made or whatever
it is but you know they're they're just they're

00:04:45.449 --> 00:04:51.860
just voices they're just it's just noise so
you have to always kind of battle through

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:57.350
that and you know if you're really struggling
with something you can always get you know

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:02.330
people to read it or you can talk to your
friends or whoever about you know the idea

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:08.139
and usually you'll get back you know positive
feedback or if you or if you're getting if

00:05:08.139 --> 00:05:13.559
you think your your script is going astray
or it's not working you can talk talk to people

00:05:13.559 --> 00:05:17.900
about it and if it actually is doing pretty
good and you're just kind of creating this

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:22.470
own you know self-doubt in your head you'll
get realigned by your friends who are like

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:26.309
no no this is pretty good so far or like maybe
they'll read the first you know whatever first

00:05:26.309 --> 00:05:30.021
act and they're like nah I really enjoyed
it and so that that positive feedback can

00:05:30.021 --> 00:05:35.789
help sort of reset you can you talk about
a time when fear won out and you did not finish

00:05:35.789 --> 00:05:43.490
the screenplay I think that there has been
times that yeah I think I have had time for

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:50.309
I'm writing something and working on an idea
and for whatever reason you know you just

00:05:50.309 --> 00:05:55.409
get to a point where you you maybe it's not
fear necessarily you know because I don't

00:05:55.409 --> 00:06:01.249
know if I'm like afraid of the potential of
this idea or if it's that I'm afraid of of

00:06:01.249 --> 00:06:09.360
this idea not being well received but but
for whatever reason you're you might be working

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:13.389
on something you're just like yeah I just
I don't know I'm just not as passionate about

00:06:13.389 --> 00:06:20.550
this screenplay as I used to be so so maybe
it's not fear specifically at least in the

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:28.460
example that I'm thinking of for myself but
yeah I don't know

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:32.380
is finishing things no matter what not not
just a screenplay is that something that's

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:38.599
always been instilled in you I don't know
if I don't know if it's like necessarily like

00:06:38.599 --> 00:06:44.449
an adage that I subscribe to I think i've
just probably I don't know where I got it

00:06:44.449 --> 00:06:49.379
or I got this sound bite but you know i've
heard someone say that it's like you know

00:06:49.379 --> 00:06:55.429
you know anyone can start writing a book or
a screenplay or whatever but it takes you

00:06:55.429 --> 00:07:02.490
know real dedication to finish something and
that's not to say that I'm like you know the

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:08.089
the pillar of that you know platitude that
you know I'm I'm I always finish everything

00:07:08.089 --> 00:07:12.710
that I start but but I I found that quote
to be you know inspiring to myself and so

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:17.439
I'm like yeah like that is true like it really
does take a lot of work to actually finish

00:07:17.439 --> 00:07:24.869
something and that alone kind of makes it
worth doing because not a lot of people can't

00:07:24.869 --> 00:07:30.740
sit through it all the way so I try to try
to remind myself of that from time to time

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:35.849
as I'm working if that's the thing that helps
motivate me to keep going then i'll definitely

00:07:35.849 --> 00:07:42.490
channel that idea how did you develop your
writing routine it took a few years to figure

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:49.860
out what kind of worked for me I used to be
someone who would just power like you know

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:54.479
do like eight nine hour sessions and just
keep going or I would right in the middle

00:07:54.479 --> 00:07:59.469
of the night because I felt like I was more
creative then or whatever it was but over

00:07:59.469 --> 00:08:05.789
time to me I found that the most productive
thing for me to do was to have it be scheduled

00:08:05.789 --> 00:08:11.349
at the same time and to just do it for you
know three or four hours max because after

00:08:11.349 --> 00:08:17.119
about three or four hours if I'm actually
doing the work correctly I'm pretty spent

00:08:17.119 --> 00:08:23.099
after that like or I feel like there's diminishing
returns after a while but I also did pick

00:08:23.099 --> 00:08:28.389
up a tip that I applied to myself and it really
worked for me as I was watching an interview

00:08:28.389 --> 00:08:33.570
with taika watit I actually and he was talking
about his writing process and he was being

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:38.030
you know a reverend you know towards himself
and self-deprecating is talking about how

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:42.330
he has add or whatever and he was like you
know what really helps him is that he actually

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:49.241
sets a timer on his desk and he writes in
20 minute windows so he will do a timer that

00:08:49.241 --> 00:08:52.690
so like you have to write for 20 minutes and
there's no internet there's no checking your

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:57.451
phone there's no whatever and then you get
five minutes after that 20 minutes and you

00:08:57.451 --> 00:09:01.940
can do whatever you want you can browse reddit
you can go on youtube you can go watch a film

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:06.780
courage video whatever you want to do and
then the clock goes back to 20 minutes and

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:11.310
you got to write again and then you can take
another five minute break and I actually started

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:17.900
doing that for myself and it was super super
effective because it just it just forces you

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:25.260
to either stare at that blank page with a
cursor blinking or you or you have to or or

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:28.890
you just you know start typing it's like you
know because there's always that instinct

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:33.370
to like pick up your phone when you don't
know what's right next or you know you're

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:38.270
hung up on a word or a sentence or a line
of dialogue and you know it can become very

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:46.520
easy to tab over to a web browser and start
you know looking up cat videos but when you

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:51.900
have this feeling of a timer that's telling
you this is your time to write and you're

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:57.220
not allowed to do anything else that little
added pressure is really helpful for avoiding

00:09:57.220 --> 00:10:03.010
distractions so that was something that he
mentioned that he did and ever since I tried

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:08.080
it out i've definitely built that into my
routine and it's worked really well was that

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:12.940
his ted talk you know it might have been okay
I'm not really sure it was it was definitely

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:18.020
an interview of some kind that I happened
to catch and maybe it was a ted talk or maybe

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:25.970
it was just a you know talking behind the
scenes or a promotion for a film that he did

00:10:25.970 --> 00:10:30.140
I'm not really sure where it was that I saw
that it's interesting too because it almost

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:35.510
reminds me of how some of these tech companies
give people like an hour free time to be on

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:41.380
the to to surf the net and just just so they
don't feel like they have to be stuck at their

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:46.550
desk and I'm wondering if we almost need that
like because then we feel too like okay this

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:50.870
is a chore you have to do this yeah actually
that's a great point because there is that

00:10:50.870 --> 00:10:56.650
sensation of like you know we all do it we
all like go on whatever facebook or instagram

00:10:56.650 --> 00:11:01.010
and you all kind of noodle around while you're
working and then there's this feeling of a

00:11:01.010 --> 00:11:06.350
little bit of a guilt that you're procrastinating
but if if you have these windows these time

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:09.090
slots where it's like okay 20 minutes work
and then now five minutes you can do whatever

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:13.830
you want you can watch all the you know cat
videos you want in this five minute time it

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:19.340
actually is very freeing and you get you know
you give yourself that permission to procrastinate

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:22.200
in that little window and yeah I find it really
helpful.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:23.200
Question For The Viewers:

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:23.206
What helps you finish your screenplays?

